## Custom configuration
{{- end -}}

{{- define "custom.custom.configuration" -}}
{{ template "custom.custom.configuration.header" . }}

### Persistence

Home Assistant runs as uid 1000. Without `persistence.config` the chart mounts
an emptyDir at `/config`, so the configuration is lost when the pod is
recreated. For real use give it a volume:

```yaml
persistence:
  config:
    enabled: true
    existingClaim: home-assistant-config
```

### Networking: host network or Multus

By default the pod uses `hostNetwork: true`, so discovery (mDNS, SSDP,
HomeKit) works on the node's LAN. To give Home Assistant its own address on
the LAN instead, attach a second interface with
[Multus](https://github.com/k8snetworkplumbingwg/multus-cni) and turn host
networking off. Set it on the `main` controller rather than in
`defaultPodOptions`, so a bundled database pod does not get the same address.

```yaml
controllers:
  main:
    pod:
      hostNetwork: false
      dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst
      annotations:
        k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: |
          [{"name": "lan", "namespace": "kube-system",
            "ips": ["192.168.1.25/24"], "mac": "c6:5e:a4:00:00:25"}]
```

A fixed MAC keeps the DHCP reservation and router rules stable across pod
restarts. A matching macvlan `NetworkAttachmentDefinition` (static IPs need
the `static` IPAM plugin, or `host-local` with a range):

```yaml
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata:
  name: lan
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  config: |
    {
      "cniVersion": "0.3.1",
      "type": "macvlan",
      "master": "eth0",
      "mode": "bridge",
      "capabilities": {"ips": true, "mac": true},
      "ipam": {"type": "static", "routes": [{"dst": "0.0.0.0/0", "gw": "192.168.1.1"}]}
    }
```

### Prometheus

Home Assistant exposes metrics at `/api/prometheus` once the
[Prometheus integration](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/prometheus/)
is enabled; scraping needs a long-lived access token. With the Prometheus
Operator:

```yaml
serviceMonitor:
  main:
    service:
      identifier: main
    endpoints:
      - port: http
        path: /api/prometheus
        interval: 1m
        bearerTokenSecret:
          name: home-assistant-prometheus   # key "token": a long-lived access token
          key: token
```

### Recorder database

The chart can run the recorder database next to Home Assistant on the official
`mariadb` or `postgres` image (enable one of them). It creates a StatefulSet,
a Service and a Secret, and sets `RECORDER_DB_URL` on the Home Assistant
container. Point the recorder at it in `configuration.yaml`:

```yaml
recorder:
  db_url: !env_var RECORDER_DB_URL
```

```yaml
mariadb:
  enabled: true
  auth:
    existingSecret: home-assistant-db   # keys: password, root-password
```

Pinned versions follow what Home Assistant supports: MariaDB on an LTS series
(10.11, 11.4, 11.8 or 12.3) and PostgreSQL 15 or newer. Renovate keeps the
images within MariaDB 12.3 and PostgreSQL 18; moving to a new PostgreSQL major
needs a dump and restore. The password is embedded in `RECORDER_DB_URL`, so it
must be URL-safe.

### InfluxDB

The chart no longer bundles InfluxDB. Run it as its own release, for example
with InfluxData's chart, pinning the image to a current 2.x release (the
chart's default image is older):

```bash
helm repo add influxdata https://helm.influxdata.com
helm install influxdb influxdata/influxdb2 --set image.tag=2.9.1
```

Then configure Home Assistant's [InfluxDB integration](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/influxdb/)
against that service (`api_version: 2`, organization, bucket and token).

**Upgrading from 3.x:** the `influxdb` values were removed together with the
Bitnami subchart. An existing InfluxDB release from the subchart is deleted on
upgrade; export its data first (`influx backup`) and restore it into the new
release.

**Upgrading from 2.x:** the Bitnami `mariadb` and `postgresql` subcharts were
removed (Bitnami's free images are only published as `latest`). Their values
and data layout are not compatible; dump the database before upgrading and
restore it into the new StatefulSet.
